From b00029abd9930b601bef1dd50d0dcdf907147a34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Silas=20M=C3=BCller?= <github@silasmueller.de>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 09:47:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] nvidia, nvidia-modeset: hold a video memory reserve for
 scanout surfaces
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A scanout surface is physically contiguous video memory with no fallback:
nvidia-drm falls back to system memory only for NO_SCANOUT buffers.  A
process that sizes its pools to the free video memory, such as a game
sizing them to the Vulkan memory budget, can leave no room for the next
scanout surface.  The refused allocation leaves the client that asked for
it, such as Xwayland allocating through nvidia-drm, without a buffer, and
the display stops updating.

Let the display driver declare a reserve.  NVKMS knows what it scans out:
per active head it counts five scanout surfaces, each the larger of an
XRGB8888 surface of the head's ViewPortIn and the largest surface bound to
one of its layers, and four cursors of the maximum cursor size.  It sends
the sum and the largest surface size to RM with the new kernel-privileged
control NV0073_CTRL_CMD_SYSTEM_SET_DISPLAY_RESERVE after every modeset,
grows the reserve after a flip that binds a larger surface, and declares
zero when it tears down its displays.  The nvidia-modeset parameter
display_reserve_mib selects this (-1), a fixed size in MiB, or nothing (0,
the default).

RM holds the reserve as runs of PMA pages, one per surface with the page
size RM gives such a surface, plus one run for the remainder.  The runs
leave PMA's free count, so processes see less free video memory; they are
not RM objects and are not charged to any cgroup.

The reserve serves the scanout allocations of kernel clients (kernel
privilege, ISO, physically contiguous, not NO_SCANOUT): NVKMS's own, and
those nvidia-drm makes for its GEM objects, including dumb buffers, for any
client of its render node.  A compositor's framebuffers allocated by a
user-mode driver are not served.  When PMA refuses such an allocation in
_vidmemPmaAllocate, the smallest held run that covers it is freed and the
allocation is retried.  Allocations through the RM API hold the API lock
for writing unless RmRoApiLock enables read-only allocations, so no other
such allocation can take the freed pages first; UVM allocates from PMA
directly and may, and then the next covering run is freed.  When such an
allocation is freed while the reserve lacks a run of its exact shape,
vidmemPmaFree hands its pages to the reserve instead of to PMA, so they
never become free in between.

A served allocation schedules a refill one second later, and a refill that
falls short retries on a backoff of one to thirty seconds.  A new
declaration re-cuts the reserve: held runs of a new run's shape move over,
and the others stay as old runs, which still serve, until the new runs hold
enough without them.  Refills run as a work item with the API lock and
without the GPU lock, only at full GPU power with PMA initialized; with MIG
enabled the reserve holds nothing.  The runs stay held over suspend and
GC6/RTD3 and are freed at unload, before the scrubber is destroyed.  Runs
the reserve allocated are never mapped or written and are freed without
scrubbing; runs taken back from a freed allocation are scrubbed when they
are freed, as the original free would have scrubbed them.

Signed-off-by: Silas Müller <github@silasmueller.de>
